J1. Uniform marked-plaque coordinates (pp.187–188), in arbitrary p,q. Fix a₀ and work in a relatively compact ambient ball inside Ω on which the common graph radii and C^{n,α} bounds are fixed. Continuity and complementarity of the two tangent bundles give a uniform lower bound for their angle after shrinking that ball: choose complementary coordinate frames at a₀, project bases of E₁(a),E₂(a) into them and invert the resulting matrices; continuity preserves invertibility with bounded inverse. The resulting a-dependent linear map L_a and its inverse are uniformly bounded. Translate a to zero and apply L_a so the two marked tangents are the coordinate blocks. The plaques through zero have graphs (x,s_a(x)) and (u_a(y),y), with s_a(0)=u_a(0)=0 and Ds_a(0)=Du_a(0)=0. Shrink their common domain until the first derivatives have norm ≤ε, uniformly in a. This follows from the assumed continuity of the first leaf derivatives on local common-radius graph charts, not from any differentiability in a transverse variable. Set Ψ_a(x,y)=(x+u_a(y),s_a(x)+y). It has exactly the marked plaques as its axes. Write Ψ_a=id+R_a. With the product norm, ||DR_a||≤ε<1/4. Apply the ACTUAL written Lipschitz inverse algorithm of Glöckner2007 arXiv math/0511218v2, Theorem5.3/Remark5.6, printed pp41–43, on the common open ball B_r(0) in the finite-dimensional REAL Banach space E=R^p times R^q with this SAME product maximum norm. The fixed linear comparison map is A=id_E, for every centre a. This is a quantitative local inverse application, not a smooth transverse foliation chart or a claim of transverse C^{n,alpha} continuity.

