Here is the printed strict-interior closed-ball construction, with its actual carrier and guards. For any target w with ||w||<(1-epsilon)r, choose 0<t<1 so that ||w||<(1-epsilon)tr. The closed ball C_t={v:||v||<=tr} lies INSIDE B_r(0). The SAME real product maximum-norm E is complete by mathlib.finite_dimensional_complete. Its norm is continuous: apply mathlib.continuousOn_norm to the identity on the whole space. Hence C_t is closed by mathlib.isClosed_le against the constant tr, and its induced subtype metric is complete by mathlib.isClosed_completeSpace_coe_g7. This uses completeness of a closed subset, NOT compactness of a Banach-space closed ball. It is nonempty, with marked point0, because tr>0. On C_t use the source's map T_{a,w}(v)=v-A^{-1}(Psi_a(v)-w)=w-R_a(v). Since R_a(0)=0, the same two-point bound gives ||R_a(v)||<=epsilon||v|| and ||T_{a,w}(v)||<=||w||+epsilon tr<tr. Thus T_{a,w} is an actual self-map of C_t, and its two-point Lipschitz constant is at most epsilon<1. Apply g9.mathlib_contracting_exists_fixedPoint to this complete metric subtype with initial point0; its initial distance is finite. Its fixed point v lies in C_t, and the equation v=w-R_a(v) gives Psi_a(v)=w. Uniqueness follows from mathlib.contracting_fixedPoint_unique_g10, or from the already proved injectivity. Therefore B_{(1-epsilon)r}(0) is contained in Psi_a(B_r(0)) for EVERY a, and the inverse I_a on this common target ball is the unique inverse of the SAME Psi_a.

The printed Theorem5.3(d)/Remark5.6 equation(66) repeats that closed-ball argument at any source point y, using A=id and a radius s with 0<s<=r-||y||: Psi_a(B_s(y)) contains B_{(1-epsilon)s}(Psi_a(y)) and is contained in B_{(1+epsilon)s}(Psi_a(y)). The first inclusion again uses a STRICT target inequality and a smaller closed ball contained in the original open domain. Thus every point of the actual image has an ambient image neighborhood; the image is open, and the lower two-point estimate makes Psi_a a homeomorphism onto that image. These source inclusions also give smaller common source/receiving radii and the common inverse Lipschitz bound needed below.

Finally apply the ACTUAL topological-parameter proof of Theorem5.10(a-c), printed pp45–46. Let P be the current centre neighborhood with its original topology; the marked graph-value maps u_a(y),s_a(x), and hence (a,z)↦Psi_a(z), are jointly continuous on the SAME P times B_r(0) by the given common-radius graph/first-jet continuity and the preceding continuous L_a frame choice. No derivative in a is assumed. The fixed A=id and uniform two-point remainder bound are exactly its premises. Put W=union_{a in P}{a} times Psi_a(B_r(0)) and define I(a,w)=I_a(w). To see its source proof operate, take (a,w) in W and y=I_a(w). For any sufficiently small s>0 with s<=r-||y||, continuity of the graph values gives a parameter neighborhood Q on which ||Psi_b(y)-Psi_a(y)||<(1-epsilon)s/2. For b in Q and ||w'-w||<(1-epsilon)s/2, the source image-ball inclusion puts w' in Psi_b(B_s(y)); hence I_b(w') is defined and ||I_b(w')-y||<s. This proves W open and I jointly continuous, exactly as the printed proof. Consequently (a,z)↦(a,Psi_a(z)) is a homeomorphism P times B_r(0)→W with inverse (a,w)↦(a,I_a(w)). Restricting that SAME inverse to P times B_{(1-epsilon)r}(0) supplies common inverse/image radii and joint inverse continuity. Only parts(a-c) of5.10 are used: neither its optional parameter Lipschitz clause nor the differentiable-parameter Theorem5.13 is invoked.

